fnm source package
==================

fnm is third-party upstream (https://github.com/Schniz/fnm); only debian/
is ours. Two upstream tarballs, both built by debian/make-orig-tarballs.sh
from an upstream git clone at the release tag:

 * fnm_<ver>.orig.tar.xz: the repository at the tag.
 * fnm_<ver>.orig-vendor.tar.xz: every crate in Cargo.lock, unpacked to
   vendor/. Launchpad's builders have no network. Crates that only build
   for Windows, macOS or WebAssembly are reduced to their Cargo.toml and
   an empty lib.rs.

debian/patches moves Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock off the released versions
to clear crate advisories, and the build runs cargo with --frozen, so the
script applies the series before vendoring: a vendor set made from the
released lock holds the wrong versions and fails the build. Refreshing the
lock means regenerating both the vendor tarball and debian/copyright, so
re-run the script after touching a patch that moves it.

The script also regenerates the vendor/ paragraphs of debian/copyright.
It takes an upstream clone and a tag, because the tracked part of this tree
is debian/ and .forgejo/ only - the upstream source beside them is a working
copy that .gitignore keeps out of git, and it may be absent entirely in a
fresh clone. Run it from the packaging tree:

  debian/make-orig-tarballs.sh ../dl-fnm/src v<ver>

.forgejo/registry-upload does the same thing in CI, cloning the tag the
changelog names into a temporary directory, so a release needs no clone on
disk.

One debian/ serves noble, resolute and stonking. The committed changelog
entry carries the packaging version alone (<ver>-1~ppaN, UNRELEASED) and
whatever builds it appends the series: .forgejo/registry-upload for the
registry, mksrc-series when building for Launchpad. fnm is a compiled
binary, so each series is built separately - one build cannot serve all
three the way an Architecture: all package can.

Upstream pins Rust 1.88, which on noble comes from the versioned
cargo-1.91/rustc-1.91 packages in noble-updates (see debian/rules). CI puts
that toolchain on PATH for cargo vendor as well as for the build.

Upstream's test suite (cargo test and the e2e tests) downloads Node.js
releases from nodejs.org, so the package build does not run it; it
smoke-tests the built binary instead.

